## What I did

Continued defect #1, `D-FOREIGN-REPLAY-UNGATED-IMAGES`, at step 4a — implementing items 1–4 of the 6-item edit list from the sess52 GPT ruling. Build is clean.

**Item 1 — `dlm/dlm_caw.{c,h}`, adopt-CAS.** `caw_adopt_retained()` converts the two `mxfs_dlm_caw_lock` fast paths from read-and-return into a real CAS whenever they find one of our bits that this incarnation never tracked. That is what serializes adoption against the settle purge — both sides now write the same slot, so the loser re-reads instead of one of them winning silently. `ex_grant_epoch` is inherited, not restamped.

**Item 1b — closed GPT's "tracking must not be best-effort" hole differently than proposed.** GPT wanted a failed `track_held` to fail the acquisition. That would abort a mount-time AG acquire and kill recovery. Instead the overflow latches `ctx->held_overflow`, which makes `SKIP_TRACKED` purging refuse with `-EOVERFLOW` for the rest of the mount. Same invariant — a live hold can never be purged — but it fails toward leaking slots rather than toward stripping authority.

**Items 2–3 — `dlm/v5_mount.c` mount path.** Step 4 now retains the EX/PW manifest and opens the adopt window; step 6.5 records the stale peer mask + per-slot node ids instead of purging them.

**Item 4 — the settle.** Factored the D2 tail of `v5_lease_expire_cb` into `v5_start_slice_recovery()`, then built `mxfs_v5_dlm_mount_settle()`: own-slot `SKIP_TRACKED` reclaim → close the window (in that order — reversing it re-opens the race) → re-verify each deferred stale slot over the full 62 s dead threshold *and* an identity check → fence → slice recovery.

## Finding that changed the design

The disklock monitor never fires for slots that were already frozen when we mounted: `check_dead` is gated on `nt->live`, and `nt->live` requires first observing that heartbeat *advance*. So step 6.5's purge was the only thing handling previous-crashed-instance locks — and it purged without replaying, the same D2 violation sess9 fixed on the lease path. Settle phase 3 is required, not redundant; deleting 6.5's purge alone would have re-created the stuck-orphan mount deadlock its own comment warns about.

Related: `get_stale_slot_mask`'s liveness test requires the node id to match, so a slot re-claimed mid-window reads as falsely stale. Phase 3 therefore gates fencing on an explicit identity check — without it the settle could hardware-fence a healthy new occupant.

## Remaining

Items 5–6: the `mxfs_dlm_mount_recovery_settle(mp)` durability barrier in `xfs/xfs_mxfs_dlm.c` and its call site in `pal/linux/xfs_super.c`. The tree is at a point where mount-path behavior has changed but nothing closes the window yet — items 5–6 must land (and VERSION rev to 0.11.399) before boarding. Details in ccmemory as `ccloop-c7ee71c6-sess53-step4a-items1-4-shipped`.

**Criteria: NO.** Not production ready — 11 open defects, 4 critical, and #1 is mid-implementation.

